    {
      "type": "NARRATE",
      "text": "Welcome to Qualution Quantum Academy. In classical computation, information is fundamentally stored as binary digits, or bits.",
      "duration": 5.0
    },
    {
      "type": "CAPTION",
      "id": "cap-bit-intro",
      "text": "A classical bit is the basic atomic unit of classical information."
    },
    {
      "type": "WRITE",
      "id": "state-heading",
      "text": "1. Distinct Binary States:",
      "x": 160,
      "y": 180,
      "duration": 1.2,
      "style": {
        "fontSize": 26,
        "fontWeight": "600",
        "color": "#E2E8F0"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "DRAW",
      "id": "box-state-0",
      "shape": "rect",
      "x": 160,
      "y": 230,
      "w": 180,
      "h": 100,
      "filled": true,
      "duration": 1.0,
      "style": {
        "color": "#64748B",
        "strokeWidth": 2.0
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "WRITE",
      "id": "label-state-0",
      "text": "STATE 0 (OFF)",
      "x": 180,
      "y": 270,
      "duration": 1.0,
      "style": {
        "fontSize": 20,
        "fontWeight": "bold",
        "color": "#94A3B8"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "DRAW",
      "id": "box-state-1",
      "shape": "rect",
      "x": 420,
      "y": 230,
      "w": 180,
      "h": 100,
      "filled": true,
      "duration": 1.0,
      "style": {
        "color": "#38BDF8",
        "strokeWidth": 2.5
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "WRITE",
      "id": "label-state-1",
      "text": "STATE 1 (ON)",
      "x": 445,
      "y": 270,
      "duration": 1.0,
      "style": {
        "fontSize": 20,
        "fontWeight": "bold",
        "color": "#38BDF8"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "NARRATE",
      "text": "At any point in time, a classical bit can exist in only one discrete state: definitively zero, or definitively one.",
      "duration": 5.2
    },
    {
      "type": "HIGHLIGHT",
      "id": "hl-state-1",
      "x": 410,
      "y": 220,
      "width": 200,
      "height": 120,
      "color": "rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.2)"
    },
    {
      "type": "WRITE",
      "id": "math-heading",
      "text": "2. Mathematical Representation:",
      "x": 160,
      "y": 380,
      "duration": 1.2,
      "style": {
        "fontSize": 26,
        "fontWeight": "600",
        "color": "#E2E8F0"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "MATH",
      "id": "math-classical-bit",
      "expression": "b \\in \\{0, 1\\} \\quad \\text{where state is mutually exclusive}",
      "x": 160,
      "y": 440,
      "duration": 1.8,
      "style": {
        "scale": 1.2,
        "fontSize": 24,
        "color": "#F5C842"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "NARRATE",
      "text": "Mathematically, b belongs to the discrete set zero or one. There is no in-between state in a classical transistor.",
      "duration": 5.0
    },
    {
      "type": "WRITE",
      "id": "summary-text",
      "text": "Deterministic • Single Value • Macro Voltage Level",
      "x": 160,
      "y": 520,
      "duration": 1.4,
      "style": {
        "fontSize": 24,
        "color": "#34D399"
      }
